## How it works
`src/lib/auth.ts` sends a magic link and sets an HTTP-only session cookie.

```ts src/lib/auth.ts
export async function sendMagicLink(email: string) {
  const token = await issueToken(email);
  await mail.send(email, linkFor(token));
}
```

## Extending it
Add a role to `session.roles`; never read the cookie in a component.

> [!WARNING] The cookie is HTTP-only on purpose. Reading it in a component breaks the job check.

## Before
```ts Before: the form forgets
catch (e) {
  setError("Something went wrong");
}
```

## After
```ts After: the form keeps your work
catch (e) {
  setError("You're offline. Your details are kept; try again.");
  keepValues(form);
}
```

## The change
```diff
 catch (e) {
-  setError("Something went wrong");
+  setError("You're offline. Your details are kept; try again.");
+  keepValues(form);
 }
```

## Why
- A generic error gives the person nothing to do next.
- Clearing the form after a network failure loses their work.
- The locked test M3-R1-S2 checks both the message and the kept values.

## The schema
```ts
export const Signup = z.object({
  name: z.string().trim().min(1, "Enter your name"),
  email: z.email("Enter an email like name@studio.com"),
});
```

## On the server
```ts
const parsed = Signup.safeParse(await request.json());
if (!parsed.success) return fieldErrors(parsed.error);
```

## Why it's written this way
- The messages say how to fix the field, not just that it's wrong.
- The server never trusts the browser's check.

